Security: One event with endless recurrences no longer breaks the whole calendar

A single event whose recurrences exceed what Sabre expands while filtering
made every time-range query of its calendar fail with HTTP 500
(MaxInstancesExceededException). Sabre counts the recurrences from DTSTART,
so this hit not only FREQ=MINUTELY but also every daily series older than
about ten years or weekly series older than about 67 years. No event of that
calendar could be loaded any more, and in a calendar shared with write access
any member could make it unusable for everybody.

For such an object only the time-range condition is now taken as met: the
other filters of the query (prop-filter with text-match, is-not-defined,
comp-filter) are still applied, so a search for a summary or for events
without RRULE leaves it out when it does not match. Where possible the
database query has already limited it to the requested time range; a series
that merely lies in the range but has no instance in it (a Monday series
queried for a Tuesday) is still returned, and clients such as the calendar app
expand the recurrences themselves. This is logged on level debug only, as it
happens on every view change of such a calendar. The other events of the
calendar are returned as before.

Known limitation: a calendar-query or calendar-multiget that asks the server
to expand the recurrences (`<C:expand>` in `<C:calendar-data>`) and a
free-busy-query REPORT over such an event still fail with HTTP 500. Sabre
expands the event there after the query; handling it would mean replacing
Sabre's report handlers. The calendar app uses none of these requests.
